.\" Man page for CleeCode. Rendered with: man docs/clee.1
.\" Kept in roff by hand rather than generated: it is short, and a generator would be one more
.\" thing to keep in step with the key bindings.
.TH CLEE 1 "August 2026" "CleeCode 0.12.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clee \- terminal IDE with a file tree, embedded terminals and syntax highlighting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B clee
.RI [ FILE | DIRECTORY ]
.br
.B clee
.BR \-w " " \fINAME\fR
.br
.B clee
.BR \-e " " \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B clee
.RB [ \-\-install\-font ]
.RB [ \-\-install\-app ]
.RB [ \-\-resume ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-V ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B CleeCode
puts an editor, a file tree and real terminals in one window. The terminals are proper ptys, so
.BR ssh (1),
.BR vim (1)
and anything else interactive works inside them.
.PP
Everything is reachable from the keyboard; the mouse is an alternative, never the only way. With
no argument the last project folder and its open files are restored. A directory argument becomes
the project root; a file argument is opened in the current directory. A bare
.B clee
never reopens a saved workspace \(em that is always a deliberate act, with
.B \-w
or the Workspace menu.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e " " \fIFILE\fR ", " \-\-edit " " \fIFILE\fR
Open just that file: the editor and nothing else, with no sidebar, terminals or menu bar. Leaves
your saved layout and session untouched, so a quick edit does not become the state you come back
to.
.TP
.BR \-w " " \fINAME\fR ", " \-\-workspace " " \fINAME\fR
Open a saved workspace: its project root, open files, frame sizes and terminals, with each
shell's startup command run for it. With no
.I NAME
it lists the workspaces you have and exits.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-font
Install the bundled CleeCodeMono Nerd Font for the current user, so the file tree's icons render.
Restart the terminal afterwards.
.TP
.B \-\-install\-app
macOS only. Build a
.B CleeCode.app
launcher in
.IR /Applications ,
or in
.I ~/Applications
when the first is not writable, so CleeCode can sit in the Dock and be chosen as the application
that opens a file or a folder. The bundle is built locally rather than downloaded, which is what
keeps it free of Gatekeeper's quarantine; it needs
.BR ghostty (1)
to host the editor, and has the path to
.B clee
compiled into it, so run this again after moving the binary. Uninstall by dragging the app to the
Bin.
.TP
.B \-\-resume
Start in the project last worked in, whatever directory the command was run from. This is what
the Dock launcher uses, because an icon has no current directory; a bare
.B clee
still opens where you are standing.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
There are no function keys and no PageUp/PageDown: on a laptop both need
.BR Fn .
There are no Alt+letter chords either, because macOS only sends Option as Meta on US keyboard
layouts, so on any other one they never arrived at all.
.PP
.B Ctrl+Shift
is the application's own layer. It is safe inside a terminal for a structural reason: no terminal
can encode Ctrl+Shift for the program running in a pane, so nothing there is listening for it.
Every other Ctrl chord belongs to a focused shell \(em Ctrl+J is Enter there, Ctrl+E is
end\-of\-line.
.SS Moving around
.TP
.B Ctrl+Alt+\(<-\(ua\(da\(->
Go to the frame in that direction: the sidebar, either half of a split editor, or a tiled
terminal, whichever is actually there. Ctrl with an arrow alone is taken by macOS for Mission
Control and Spaces, which is why Alt joins in.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Tab ", " Ctrl+Shift+Tab
Cycle the frames, the way Cmd+Tab cycles windows.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+\(<-/\(->
Previous or next tab of whichever frame has focus.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+\(ua/\(da
Previous or next terminal window.
.SS Application
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+M
The built\-in manual, in English or Italian.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+B
Open the menu bar; then arrows, Enter, and the underlined letter to jump to a menu.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+O
Settings.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+G
Context menu for the focused frame. Right\-click does the same.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+R
Run the current file. The toolbar button beside \fBRun\fR sets what it uses: the Python venv
on a .py file, the \fB[run_commands]\fR entry for any other extension.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+U
Resize mode: arrows grow the focused frame, Shift+arrow shrinks it, Esc or Enter leaves.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+P ", " Ctrl+O
Command palette and quick open, both fuzzy. Nothing needs to be memorised.
.SS Files and editing
.TP
.BR Ctrl+S ", " Ctrl+Shift+S
Save, save all. An unnamed buffer asks for a name.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+W ", " Ctrl+D
Close the current tab, prompting if it is unsaved.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Z ", " Ctrl+Y
Undo, redo.
.TP
.BR Ctrl+F ", " Ctrl+G
Find and replace, go to line.
.TP
.B Ctrl+K
Toggle a line comment.
.TP
.B Alt+\(<-/\(->
Move by word; Shift extends the selection. Alt rather than Ctrl because an Option arrow makes no
printable character, so it survives every keyboard layout.
.TP
.BR "Alt+\(ua/\(da" ", " Alt+Shift+\(da
Move the current line up or down; duplicate it.
.TP
.B Alt+drag
Column selection. It is also in the Edit menu, and from there Shift+arrows draw the rectangle.
Columns past the end of a short line are clipped, not padded.
.SS Terminals
.TP
.BR Ctrl+Shift+T ", " Ctrl+Shift+K
New tab; close this shell, taking its window with it when it was the last tab.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+N
New terminal window.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+E
Name the focused terminal and give it a startup command.
.TP
.B Shift+arrows
Select text inside a pane. The selection goes straight to the clipboard.
.TP
.B Shift+PageUp, Shift+PageDown
Move a screenful through what has scrolled off the pane. The mouse wheel does the same without
asking for Fn, and typing anything jumps back to the live output. How much is kept is
\fBterminal_scrollback\fR in
.IR settings.toml ,
2000 lines by default; a shell reads it when it starts, so a change reaches the next shell
opened rather than the ones already running. A full\-screen program (vim, less) keeps the wheel
for itself, having no history of its own to walk.
.SS Octave and Python
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+X
Send the selection, or the cell the cursor is in, to an interpreter already running in one of the
terminals. A cell runs from one \fB%%\fR line to the next \(em written \fB%%\fR in Octave and
\fB# %%\fR in Python. Nothing is pasted at the prompt: it goes through a temporary file, because
a pasted indented block makes Python answer IndentationError.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+I
Look inside a variable the session holds: its values a screenful at a time, rows and columns
numbered. Arrows page, Home returns to the corner, R asks again, Esc closes. It shows and does
not edit.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+P
Put a breakpoint on the cursor's line, or take it off. When the session reaches one it stops, the
editor marks the line, and the workspace window shows the frame's own variables rather than the
session's. Stepping is typed at the prompt where the session is waiting \(em
.B dbstep
and
.B dbcont
in Octave,
.B n
and
.B c
at Python's (Pdb) \(em and the status line says which. Nothing is typed at your prompt to set a
breakpoint or to read anything back.
.SS File tree
.RB \(bu " n" " / " N
new file or folder \(bu
.B e
rename \(bu
.B Delete
remove \(bu
.B H
show hidden files \(bu
.B Enter
open a file, or make a folder the project root.
.SH WORKSPACES
A workspace is a whole set\-up saved under a name: project root, open files, frame sizes, and the
terminal windows with their tab names and startup commands. Reopening one brings the shells back
already running whatever they were for.
.PP
Each is one hand\-editable TOML file, so they can be copied between machines or kept in version
control. A built\-in
.B "Default layout"
is always offered and cannot be deleted or overwritten: it is the layout to go back to when the
frames have wandered.
.SH PICTURES
A picture \(em .png, .jpg, .jpeg, .gif, .webp, .bmp, .ico, .tiff \(em opens in a tab that draws
it, rather than the blank read\-only buffer a binary file gives. There is nothing in one to edit,
so no buffer is made for it.
.PP
On a terminal with a graphics protocol (kitty, iTerm2, sixel) those are real pixels; elsewhere it
falls back to coloured half\-blocks. The status line names which was used when the tab opens.
CleeCode writes the graphics itself rather than leaving it to a program in a terminal pane \(em a
pane is parsed into cells and repainted, and graphics escapes do not survive that \(em which is
also why this works over
.BR ssh (1).
.PP
Decoding happens on a background thread, so opening a large picture never stalls the window or
the shells in it. In split view a picture opens in the pane that is not focused, leaving the file
being worked on visible; the layout itself is never rearranged. Pressing \fBRun\fR on one
still runs its \fB[run_commands]\fR entry, which by default shows it in a terminal with
.BR chafa (1).
.SH MARKDOWN
A markdown file opens as text, since that is what you do with one. \fBRun\fR beside it shows it
rendered in the other half of the editor, splitting it if it was not already.
.PP
Two tabs, one file, one copy of the text: the rendered tab owns nothing and is a view of the
buffer being typed in, so the two cannot disagree, and closing the source closes the view. A
\fB\(bu\fR glyph tells them apart in the strip.
.PP
Where the terminal can draw pictures and
.BR pandoc (1)
is installed, the rendering is a real document \(em proper fonts, and pictures the text refers to
appearing in the flow of it, which a grid of characters cannot do. That takes around half a
second, so it follows pauses in typing rather than each keystroke. Without either, it falls back
to styled terminal text, which keeps up with the keys and works anywhere. \fBRun\fR on a rendered
tab says \fBRefresh\fR and forces it.
.SH PDFS
A PDF opens as pages, drawn the same way a picture is. The plain arrow keys turn them \(em
\fB\(<-\fR/\fB\(ua\fR back, \fB\(->\fR/\fB\(da\fR on \(em with no chord to remember, since a
preview tab has no text for a cursor to move through. The page you are on is written along the
bottom of the frame.
.PP
Pages are rasterised by
.BR pdftoppm (1)
from poppler, or failing that by
.BR gs (1).
Neither is bundled: a PDF renderer CleeCode could link is either under a licence this project
cannot take or a prebuilt binary blob per platform. With neither installed the tab says so
rather than sitting empty; any TeX distribution brings Ghostscript with it.
.PP
A PDF that changes on disk is re\-rendered in place, on the page being read \(em edit the source,
press \fBRun\fR, and the page beside it becomes the one just typeset.
.SH RUNNING CODE
The button to the left of \fBRun\fR names what \fBRun\fR would use on the file currently on
screen, and opens the menu that changes it. What that menu offers follows the file's extension,
so the button is never a control that does nothing.
.TP
.B Python files
The venv selector: the virtualenvs found in the project root, plus any registered from elsewhere
on disk. The chosen one replaces \fBpython\fR/\fBpython3\fR in the run command and is remembered
per workspace. A venv means nothing to any other interpreter, which is why it is offered nowhere
else.
.TP
.B Every file type
The last two entries edit the run command for that extension, written to \fB[run_commands]\fR in
.I settings.toml
(shared by every project) or in
.I .cleecode.toml
in the project root (that project alone, and it wins). A \(bu marks whichever one \fBRun\fR
would actually use. This is how a file type with no built\-in entry \(em .tex, .sql, .rs \(em
gets one, and how one project typesets its own master file while every other .tex keeps the
shared command. Emptying the project box drops the override; emptying the shared one restores
the built\-in default.
.PP
A command may use four placeholders, each shell\-quoted so paths with spaces survive:
.B {file}
the whole path,
.B {dir}
the folder it sits in,
.B {name}
the file name,
.B {stem}
that name without its extension. The command is typed at a real shell, so steps chain as they
read \(em
.RB ` "pdflatex {file} && open {dir}/{stem}.pdf" '.
.PP
An interpreter installed outside
.B PATH
goes under
.BR [interpreter_paths] ,
mapping a program name to an absolute path. Octave on Windows is found automatically in Program
Files, and a .m file is handed to an Octave prompt that is already open rather than starting a
second interpreter.
.SH NUMERIC SESSIONS
.B clee \-w octave
and
.B clee \-w pylab
open the interpreter already running, with a second terminal window beside it showing what the
session holds: every variable with its size, class and range, filling in by itself whenever a
command finishes. Nothing is typed at your prompt to ask \(em the session reports its own state
from an idle hook \(em so the transcript stays a record of what you did and a busy interpreter is
never interrupted.
.PP
A plot opens as a tab beside the script that drew it, and no window opens anywhere: a live figure
window cannot be moved into a terminal, so the session is told to open none and hands over a
picture. Re\-plot and the tab changes with it; a tab you close stays closed until that figure is
drawn again. On a figure tab
.BR + / \-
and the arrows move the plot by asking the session to draw it again rather than by magnifying
pixels \(em magnified pixels leave the axis labels describing a range that is no longer on screen.
.B r
restores the whole plot and
.B e
writes it out.
.PP
.B Plots as tabs
in the Run menu turns the capture off, and then Octave keeps its qt windows and matplotlib its own backend \(em
plotting behaves as it does outside CleeCode. The same switch is
.B plots_in_tabs
in
.IR settings.toml .
It applies to sessions started afterwards, since an interpreter chooses this once at startup.
Where there is no screen to put a window on the choice is not offered and the plots stay as tabs:
over ssh without X forwarding, or on a machine with no
.BR DISPLAY ,
a window has nowhere to open.
.B ssh \-X
with an X server at your end counts as a screen, so there the choice is yours.
.PP
Nothing is installed and nothing is written to your home directory: the interpreter is handed the
code and a path to write to through its environment. Python figures need matplotlib installed in
the same python the terminal runs.
.PP
Setting
.B CLEECODE_DBG_LOG
to a path makes both hooks record why they failed. Everything they do runs inside a guard that
must not break the REPL, so a mistake there has one symptom: a panel that quietly stops changing.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/cleecode/settings.toml
Preferences, layout, terminal scrollback, run commands per file extension, and interpreter
paths. On Windows,
.IR %APPDATA%\ecleecode\e .
.TP
.I ~/.config/cleecode/workspaces/
One TOML file per saved workspace.
.TP
.I <project root>/.cleecode.toml
Run commands belonging to one project, overriding the shared ones. Written when a per\-project
command is set and removed when the last one goes; meant to be committed with the project.
.TP
.I ~/.config/cleecode/panic.log
Written if something goes wrong internally. CleeCode contains such a failure rather than closing,
so a shell running in a pane is never taken down with it; this is where the detail goes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLEECODE
Set to
.B 1
in every shell CleeCode starts, so a shell rc can skip heavy startup work when it is not wanted
here \(em for example
.RB ` "if not set -q CLEECODE; fastfetch; end" '
in fish.
.TP
.B SHELL
The shell each terminal runs.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B clee
exits 0 on a normal quit, including after an internal error it had to give up on.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR micro (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR ssh (1)
.PP
The built\-in manual, Ctrl+Shift+M, covers the same ground with diagrams and stays in step with
the binary.
